ry workflows and accommodate  an additional leukemia test without  requiring  additional material  new leukemia testing from blood and bone  marrow requires sample to be processed  for many analyses including  hematopathology first my slide and smear  review  flow cytometry karyotyping fish  and molecular studies  with limited sample volumes and an  increasing number of required molecular  tests  it can be challenging to implement these  new tests especially those that require  different extraction methods  the feasibility of stretching the sample  across all of these tests  led us to evaluate pairing our own  extraction with an fda  approved idh1 idh2 test  the abbott real-time idh1 and idh2  assays use homogeneous real-time  fluorescent technology to identify  single nucleotide variants in idh1 or  idh2  this chart at the bottom of the slide  lists the variants that are detectable  by the assays  while this list is not all inclusive  inclusive it covers the  most common variant the variant calling  is performed on the abbott m2000rt  software in a proprietary algorithm that  reports a qualitative  positive or not detected result  in order to implement this testing our  workflow would have been as follows  from the blood which we'll follow here  we would need 200 microliters of blood  to extract for idh testing  200 microliters to extract for dna for  other  aml targets and one to four mils for rna  extraction for other aml targets  while this workflow is not impossible it  leaves no room for re-extraction when  samples are not ideal which is not  uncommon in this patient population  it also creates a complex workflow  requiring three independent extractions  not including the extraction required  for sequencing  for one sample of molecular testing  additionally the idh1 assay has  an additional dilution step compared to  the idh2  making downstream preparation more time  consuming and challenging  our goals were to evaluate a streamlined  workflow by standardizing