 the people in these with  these mutations have a normal karyotype  they the idh one and two mutations can  frequently co-occur with mpm1  and srsf2 and less frequently with  split-3 mutations  idh which stands for isocitrate  dehydrogenase is a gene that codes for  an enzyme which catalyzes the reaction  of isocitrate  to alpha-ketoglutarate while idh1 and  idh2  have similar enzymatic functions idh1 is  localized  to the cytoplasm while idh2  is localized to the mitochondria a  mutated idh1  and idh2 create alpha-ketoglutarate  competitor  2-hydroxyglutarate this 2-hd  alters the cellular metabolism dna  methylation and differentiation  as well as competes for the normal  function of alpha-q glutarate  mutations typically occur in enzymatic  arginine residues of idh1 and idh2 genes  although rarer idh mutations have been  identified  these mutations occur at amino acid 132  of idh1  and residues 140 and 172 of idh2  mutations are not specifically changed  to  one amino acid for example an arginine  to a serine  but there are amino acids that are more  commonly substituted as  are depicted on the left a handful of  them for idh1  and a handful of them for idh2  this data from the same five years shows  the prevalence  of specific idh variants and aml from  our institution  the idh1 variants are found in about  six to ten percent of aml patients of  these variants  an arginine to either a cysteine or a  histidine are the most  common variants eight to 19 percent of  aml patients  will harbor an idh2 mutation  approximately 80  of these reside in the r140 residue  and an arginine to glutamine is the most  prevalent variant  an arginine to lysine that's position  172 is also common  especially in the elderly population  next i'm going to present three cases  which highlight the clinical utility  of rapid idh testing at various stages  of disease progression  and i will do this in the context of  clinical guidelines  our first case is a 76 year old woman  who presented with leukocytosis and  circulating blast  on her peripheral blood smear she showed  thrombocytopenia  and 66 glass with prominent nucleoli  these blasts were positive for mpo cd13  cd33 and cd 177 by flow cytometry  these findings were diagnostic of acute  myeloid leukemia  as shown in the molecular landscape  aml is a heterogeneous disease that can  often be described as unique  as each individual patient both the  karyotyping and molecular testing can  help define the disease and the  prognosis of these patients  and in some cases define  the therapy decisions an intensive  versus a low intensity therapy or  targeted therapy if a mutation is  present  recently a recent update to the mccn  guidelines for acute leukemia recommend  molecular  and cytogenetic analyses for actionable  mutations to be expedited to a maximum  of three to five days turnaround  time this list includes idh which we  will discuss momentarily  which has targeted therapies available  if we return to our first case our  molecular findings  a conventional chromosome analysis  showed a normal female karyotype  it was negative for fish studies for  deletions and rearrangements assessed  molecular testing on the peripheral  blood revealed no  rearrangement transcripts idh variants  or flit  itds an r140q  idh2 variant and a d835 to v  swit3 mutation were identified with  targeted molecular testing  these results were later confirmed with  a larger massively parallel sequencing  panel  which also identified mpm1 and srsf2  disease-associated variants  the integration of the clinical picture  molecular findings is critical  to clinical management of aml  highlighting the need for this rapid  testing  younger patients can often receive  intensive standard chemo induction  chemotherapy  but many other patients are unable to  tolerate such treatments and instead  receive a less intensive therapy  a less intensive therapy may also  include a targeted approach  to actionable mutation in addition to  treatment decisions molecular results  may also  aid in deciding when to go to transplant  if we return to this first case study  this patient who is over the age of 60  years old was not a good candidate for  intensive therapy due to her poor  performance status  the mccn guidelines differentiate  treatment  induction strategies for those who are  not intensive therapy candidates  into groups with actionable mutations  and those with us  without an actionable mutation  per the mccn guidelines patients with a  de novo aml  and an idh 1 or 2 mutation may benefit  from venetoclax  with a hyper-methylating agent-based  therapy  given her idh2 mutation this patient was  considered an excellent candidate for  vidasa venetoclax upfront treatment  molecular idh results of this case were  returned within 48 hours  and this allowed the ability to rapidly  decide on therapy incorporating the  clinical considerations and  mutational status  due to the rapidity of testing both of  these findings integrated  highlight the utility of rapid idh  testing and induction chemotherapy  decisions  following a successful induction these  targeted therapies may also be added to  post-induction treatment plans if not  utilized up front  while testing a diagnosis is clearly  advantageous  what about testing for residual disease  or relapse  a clear picture of residual disease  testing for idh  is still being debated many idh variants  are persistent  even following therapy but recent  literature suggests that idh detection  may be an early sign of relapse  in the context of relapse recurrence and  also emergence of  de novo idh variants may be observed  nccn guidelines recommend testing for  actionable  genes including ivh1 and two idh  mutational status may  additionally aid in second line therapy  decision  this second case is a 68 year old woman  with a history of cmml  she had previously received six cycles  of videzo without a response  upon review at our institution she  presented with dysplasia of the  granulocytes and monocytes  her bone marrow biopsy was consistent  with refractory cml with increased blast  while this case is not aml it is  considered a high risk  myelocorporative neoplasm  her karyotype was normal with molecular  findings of an idh2  r172k variant no idh1 variants were  detected  this molecular finding was confirmed  later on the mps panel  that also identified an asxl1 dnmt2  and k-rest variant  as with diagnosis actionable mutations  should be determined at the time of  relapse  relapse here and the guidelines for nccn  showing that split 3 idh1 and idh2  should be  monitored therapy for relapse disease  includes clinical trials and targeted  therapy including those for idh1 and  idh2  due to the high risk disease and the  presence of idh2 variant this patient  met criteria for a clinical  trial with endocytome although  this is not aml the second case  highlights the utility of rapid testing  and relapsing disease  identifying therapeutic options  including clinical trials and targeted  therapy to determine changes  in molecular based prognosis and risk  stratification  targeted therapies against idh1 and idh2  selectively bind and impair enzymatic  activity  these decrease two hd productions and  allow more normal function of alpha-q  glutarate function  these actions also allow normal cell  metabolism  and differentiation  the idh1 inhibitor ivocytonib was  approved for use in revlaps and  refractory aml  in july of 2018 and for frontline aml  in may of 2019 the idh2 inhibitor is  currently only approved for  relapse and refractory use in aml  in a phase 1 2 study for the idh2  inhibitor  in mutated aml 19 19  of patients achieved a complete  remission with an overall survival of  19.7 months those with partial  remission had an overall survival of  14.4 a month  as shown here the median survival is 9.3  months  a phase 1 study in idh1 mutated relapse  and refractory aml  also showed promising results with  greater than 21  of patients achieving complete remission  and an overall median survival of  8.8 months this is markedly better than  those with  no response or those with other than a  complete remission  the third case is a 75 year old woman  presenting with neutropenia and  circulating blasts  her presentation and molecular studies  were performed at an outside hospital  and via sendout testing upon internal  review of the aspirate 44  blacks were observed and she was  diagnosed with aml  not otherwise specified  per the outside hospital report she had  a normal karyotype and normal fish  results  in-house molecular testing identified no  flip 3 variants  the sample for sequencing was deemed  quality not sufficient  this case presented just prior to our go  live of rapid idh testing so none was  performed  at the time of diagnosis  she underwent induction chemotherapy  followed by vidasa venetoclax  and unfortunately did not achieve  remission follow-up molecular testing at  this time  revealed again no foot 3 variant but  detected an idh1 variant  the idh1 result was confirmed by  sequencing  this case highlights the advantage of  rapid idh testing  frontline targeted idh therapy combined  with standard  induction is well tolerated in trials  the beat aml trial subsidy with idh2  inhibitors  used continuous 28 day cycles in newly  diagnosed patients  who were younger than 60 years and  achieved a complete remission of 43  the extension of the idh1 trial that led  to frontline use approval  boasted an overall response rate of near  59 percent  while we don't know for sure if the  variant was present at the disease onset  for this patient  the implementation of rapid idh testing  and an idh1 inhibitor  approved for frontline use may have been  added to therapy  had the idh variant been detected at  diagnosis  this case highlights  the ability for rapid testing to allow  frontline targeted therapy decisions  in this next part i'll discuss how we  were able to adjust  our laboratory workflows and accommodate  an additional leukemia test without  requiring  additional material  new leukemia testing from blood and bone  marrow requires sample to be processed  for many analyses including  hematopathology first my slide and smear  review  flow cytometry karyotyping fish  and molecular studies  with limited sample volumes and an  increasing number of required molecular  tests  it can be challenging to implement these  new tests especially those that require  different extraction methods  the feasibility of stretching the sample  across all of these tests  led us to evaluate pairing our own  extraction with an fda  approved idh1 idh2 test  the abbott real-time idh1 and idh2  assays use homogeneous real-time  fluorescent technology to identify  single nucleotide variants in idh1 or  idh2  this chart at the bottom of the slide  lists the variants that are detectable  by the assays  while this list is not all inclusive  inclusive it covers the  most common variant the variant calling  is performed on the abbott m2000rt  software in a proprietary algorithm that  reports a qualitative  positive or not detected result  in order to implement this testing our  workflow would have been as follows  from the blood which we'll follow here  we would need 200 microliters of blood  to extract for idh testing  200 microliters to extract for dna for  other  aml targets and one to four mils for rna  extraction for other aml targets  while this workflow is not impossible it  leaves no room for re-extraction when  samples are not ideal which is not  uncommon in this patient population  it also creates a complex workflow  requiring three independent extractions  not including the extraction required  for sequencing  for one sample of molecular testing  additionally the idh1 assay has  an additional dilution step compared to  the idh2  making downstream preparation more time  consuming and challenging  our goals were to evaluate a streamlined  workflow by standardizing  dna extraction and sample processing  between the two  assays with the hope of reducing the  sample amount and time required  the following was our proposed workflow  to standardize the protocols and dna  extraction  in this workflow from the blood we take  200 microliters  of blood for dna extraction and one to  four  mils of blood for rna extraction  these specimens in our study design were  extracted in parallel  on both methods dna was then analyzed  on the idh1 and idh2 assays  with the addition of the dilution step  to the idh2  protocol  all reactions for both idh1 and idh2  were concordant the asterisk  here represents one sample that was  analyzed at an outside laboratory  that was using the abbott idh assays  the kaya amp extraction and standardized  idh1 and idh2 protocols were then  utilized for the remainder of our  studies  archived dna with known idh status was  used to assess the agreement  of the idh1 and idh2 assays we tested  all variants that were available as  shown in the table at the bottom  and all of our specimens were concordant  between sequencing and the idh1  and sequencing and the idh2 assays  we then assessed any changes in the  limit of detection with workflow  modifications  in samples with enough material variant  allele frequencies  dilutions were made and run in  triplicate dilutions  above at and below the manufacturer lod  were evaluated  the lod was determined to be equivalent  to the manufacturer's stated lod  of at least two percent vaf  in lieu of time today other performance  characteristics are not shown  but we also evaluated precision  specificity and dna  input as part of these studies  molecular testing is essential for both  risk ratification and therapeutic  decisions and aml  diagnosis and relapse in order to  facilitate this rapid testing  we assessed workflow modifications in  idh1 and idh2 testing  we found that these modifications were  able to streamline the dna  extraction and normalize the assays  and gave concordant results with our  sequencing  it also had a comparable lod to the  original assay  these changes allow us a more cohesive  aml workflow  without requiring additional starting  samples  in this final part i want to discuss the  role of idh testing in solid tumors  and our studies to validate these  specimens  our final case is a 27 year old male who  presented with headaches  auditory hallucinations and seizures a  head ct and mri  showed a right temporal lobe mass that  was suggestive of an idh mutant glioma  in cns tumors idh variants are seen in  about  80 to 90 percent of astrocytomas and  oleodendrogliomas  and approximately 10 of glioblastomas  the most prevalent  variant in idh1 is r132h  in 2016 the world health organization  updated their guidelines for cns tumor  following histology idh mutations are  the next determining factor  in diagnostic flow charts since  immunohistochemistry typically detects  only a singular idh1 variant negative  ihc results will still need their idh  status verified  to move to diagnosis  in cns tumors idh mutated patients have  an  improved probability of survival over  those who are idh wild  type as seen in these charts  is clear that an idh mutation can then  serve in both  a diagnostic and prognostic  role in cns tumors  the molecular testing we expected from  our neuropathology colleagues were those  with very limited  materials that could not be run on  sequencing those with challenging  ihc studies and those that are truly  stat in nature  in this case of the 27 year old male ihc  was negative  for the idh1 variant r132h  the ifc also showed a positive tp53  staining and loss of atrx expression  however since this case was suggestive  of an idh  mutant astrocytoma the sample was sent  for idh12  rtpcr  since the abbott real-time idh1 and idh2  assays are only approved for blood and  bone marrow we needed to validate the  use of paraffin-embedded tissue as a  specimen type  we first evaluated the required dna  input  here a range of dna inputs were assessed  some which fell outside of the suggested  inputs for the blood and bone marrow by  manufacturer  and these graphs just show the range of  dna inputs that we used  a total of 196 idh1 reactions and 106  idh2 reactions were assessed low levels  of dna input resulted in low-level  amplification  of the internal control dna input above  5 nanograms was concordant we  determined that consistent with the  requirements for blood and bone  marrow 10 nanograms of dna input  was required for paraffin-embedded  tissue  similar to our blood and bone marrow  evaluation samples with known idh  results by sequencing were assessed  these were paraffin-embedded tissue from  brain resections  a single sample was detected on  sequencing that was not detected by pcr  noted by an asterisk here and this  variant was not one that was expected  to be detected by the pcr having an  e226k  variant this sample was excluded from  concordance calculations  which were 100 between sequencing  and the idh1 and idh2 assays  additionally comparison of  immunohistochemistry to the idh1  assay was completed since the ihc only  detects  r132h variant there were four specimens  with additional variants detected by pcr  these are represented by the four in  parentheses in the graph  and our variance r132c g  and l a single specimen with r132h  positivity by pcr was not detected on  the ihc  this specimen was reclassified as a true  false negative by ihc  and may represent a higher sensitivity  of the pcr  assay over ihd detection  similar to our previous studies vaf  solutions of paraffin-embedded tissue  specimens were run in triplicate  all of these replicas were concordant  consistent with the lod  in blood and bone marrow detection of  ivh1 variant  was 100 accurate down to at least two  percent  vas it is likely that these variants are  accurate below this level  based on these studies  returning to our case study the pcr  results were positive for an idh1  variant  of r132l which is expected to be missed  by  ihc the final integrated diagnosis was  diffuse  astrocytoma with idh mutant  this case illustrates that rtbcr can be  an aid in diagnosis in solid tumor cases  especially where ihc and pathology are  conflicting  in this last part i hope i've been able  to show you the idh testing in glioma is  beneficial for both diagnosis  and prognosis we've been able to bring  on idh1 and 2 testing for pep specimens  again without additional sample  requirements  this method can serve as improved  detection  compared to ihc where sample quality may  not be sufficient for sequencing  evaluation in solid tumors is not  necessarily limited to gliomas  emerging literature and clinical trials  consider the utility  of the idh inhibitors and other solid  tumors with idh mutation  in the future the need for this idh  testing may be greatly expanded  in conclusion idh testing is beneficial  in both liquid and solid tumors  at many stages from diagnosis to  prognostic determination  to relapse modified workflows allow a  streamlined  testing on both liquid and tissue  specimens  with minimal specimen requirements  with that i'd like to thank many members  of the molecular pathology lab  especially alicia who performed  a number of the validation experiments  i'm sophia who helped me  pull the relevant information from the  aml cases  thank you and with that i will take any  questions  thank you sarah for your informative  presentation we'll now start the q a  portion of the webinar  if you have a question you'd like to ask  please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  and we'll answer as many questions as we  have time for all right let's go ahead  and get started  okay sarah your first question is when  performing the various  analysis for aml patients what is the  timing on the various molecular results  and are you able to present the  karyotype  fish and molecular results at the same  time  that's a great question thanks for that  unfortunately  there isn't a straightforward answer the  timing of testing depends  highly on the draw and when the specimen  is received into the laboratory  typically fish flit and idh results are  rapid enough to be able to be  incorporated  into our surgical pathology reports we  aim to have  the actionable mutations reported within  three days so that the physicians can  utilize these results for clinical  management decisions  but due to the nature of testing the  karyotyping  and the sequencing results are often  reported later  after treatments may have started  great thank you okay your next question  for glioblastoma cases do you wait for  ihc results to come back  prior to performing the rtidh testing  we do wait for the ihc results for  gliomas we've really tried to refine the  use case  and ihc is the most rapid and cheapest  methodology so we typically perform that  first and then in cases that have  conflicting ihc and pathology we would  reflex those to pcr  and other cases where we might be  utilizing the rtpcr  assay would be those where there's not a  quality  efficient material for sequencing those  that might be truly statin nature  it can also be age dependent some cases  in  patients younger than 55 years old need  to have the idh mutation ruled out  and so we would perform rtbcr in some of  those cases as well  great thanks sarah okay we've got time  for one more question let's go ahead  and go with this one it says what is the  difference between the amount of tissue  needed for rt and mps testing  in pet samples  yeah so this is a multifaceted  answer um if we directly compare the  input amounts  the rt requires a minimum of 10  nanograms  while sequencing panels will typically  work  10 to 30 fold more material depending on  the level of degradation of the  paraffin embedded tissue specimen we do  have a hot  hot spot sequencing panel with optimized  lower inputs which helps to kind of  reduce the number of samples  deemed as quality not sufficient i think  in general the pcr assays are a little  bit more forgiving in terms of the  sample degradation  and tumor percentage required compared  to the sequencing assays  thank you again sarah do you have any  final comments for our audience before  we go today  uh i just want to thank everyone for  listening and for  your interesting questions and thank lab  roots and  abbott molecular for inviting me to  speak thank you  great thank you and before we go i'd  also like to thank the audience for  joining us today  any questions we did not have time for  today will be addressed by the speaker  via the contact information you provided  at the time of registration we would  like to thank you sarah for your time  today in your important research  we would also like to thank labyrinth  and our sponsor abbott molecular for  underwriting today's  educational webcast this webcast can be  viewed on demand  and labyrinths will alert you when it's  available for replay  until next time goodbye
 

